hey friends have you ever picked up your  guitar to find noises like this  really hard to listen to uh it's  terrible buzzing rattling sound that  spoils every single note  when you hear this out of your  instrument  it can be terrifying you can think  there's something terribly wrong inside  your your beautiful baby  the good news is that very often this  terrible buzzing noise can be fixed  instantly without taking your guitar to  the shop so let's learn how to fix a  buzzing guitar  so in my last video we learned all about  how to change the strings on a classical  guitar and when i was finished changing  those strings for the video i picked up  the guitar and i heard these terrible  sounds  now i've been playing plucked  instruments for a long time and i'm very  aware of the causes of these sounds and  it turns out that there are numerous  ways a acoustic guitar can buzz and by  the way this applies to  loots early guitars the orbos oods  mandolins most acoustic uh plucked  instruments but like i said earlier the  good news is that there's a lot of  really quick fixes here that will  instantly  solve the issue without you having to  spend money at a guitar repair shop  the first way is so easy to fix this  terrible sound that we're hearing that  sounds like there's something internally  broken in my instrument  is caused because i didn't clip the ends  of the strings  just the other day someone messaged me  on instagram and said help i don't know  what's going on my guitar it sounds  terrible they showed me a picture and  they had all these strings sticking out  like this and i said just trim those  strings and  the problem went away so on a nylon  string guitar you can just use a  fingernail clippers just find the really  long ends and clip them as close as you  can without disturbing the knot  so i have two strings that were touching  the top and making that really really  terrible sound  and now i predict we have no buzzing  [Music]  and it's gone another easy solution is  if you have  lots of strings long strings hanging out  the headstock that haven't yet been  trimmed  it's possible that they're touching one  of the other strings that are vibrating  and are causing the buzz so by trimming  the excess string you'll get rid of that  problem  i do like to coil them up in little  balls because  with more expensive strings you can  still salvage it but of course if it's  causing a buzz problem  just trim it off  [Music]  but if you've already trimmed the  strings at the bridge and you're still  getting a buzzing sound  well we have to look elsewhere let's  start by playing just your open strings  and maybe kind of heavy with a rest  stroke on the thumb  if those strings any of those strings  buzz then we have an issue that has to  be fixed but let's say you don't have  time to take your guitar to a repair  shop and you don't have a measuring tool  to check out your action here's a really  quick solution to help you in a pickle  let's say for example when you played  your open e string you got some sound  like this  if that's the case then we have to  address the problem at the nut  and the quick solution for this is to  grab a  thick piece of paper  not quite the thickness of a business  card but maybe thicker than your average  printer paper i'm going to use the  packaging paper from the aquila strings  i'm using because the paper is thicker  than your average  printer paper so we're going to take  kind of a long rectangle and then we're  going to fold it in half and what we're  going to do is we're going to put the  string in that little that little v  and now we have the string cradled  in this little piece of paper and now  what you can do is loosen  the e string down a bit so it's a bit  more  loose than usual  and now you're going to go towards the  nut with the string in the little cradle  and you're going to try to get into  the nut slot so underneath  the string and in between the nut slot  of the string so then once it's in there  you're going to then tune back the  string  [Music]  and now the paper has lifted the string  very slightly and maybe  you've gotten rid of  your buzz  if your buzz persists  you might have to get a thicker  piece of paper now the real trick here  is making sure the piece of paper uh  doesn't go on to the actual string it's  sitting inside the nut slot or a little  bit back in the nut slot if it's on the  string it'll mute it like this  that's no good we have to get the piece  of paper farther in the nut slot so  either grab it and then push it further  and see if you can get it  in the right place  that sounds good um or you can loosen  the string again and then see if you can  wiggle it around to find the sweet spot  i use this trick on a lot of my loots  and sometimes i have lots of pieces of  paper  in the in the nut slots now if you're  noticing that all of the open strings  are buzzing or that paper is not helping  you might need to do something more  radical  so one option that's a do-it-yourself  option would be actually to loosen all  the strings and take out the nut and  then put a piece of paper underneath a  shim so you can do that with a business  card which is like a millimeter thick  you just trace the size of the nut and  then you would lay that  down and then put the nut back down and  then put the strings on top and tighten  it back up thereby raising the entire  nut a millimeter  and seeing how it works you might have  to raise it two millimeters hopefully  not much more than that because if it  gets too high here it gets really really  hard to  fret the the notes perhaps a better  long-term option would be then to  definitely take it to a guitar repair  shop and have that make you  a brand new nut  [Music]  another really common source of buzzing  actually comes from the clothing or  jewelry that you're wearing  very often i get dressed up for a  concert and suddenly i'm wearing all  these buttons and a jacket with a button  on the sleeve and i suddenly hear this  buzzing sound that's very distracting  right before the concert  if you have a button that's accidentally  touching the guitar  on your wrist or jewelry or a necklace  that's touching it or buttons  those will vibrate with the instrument  and will cause terrible sounds so  prepare by dressing up before the  concert and getting used to it because  you might have to adjust the fabric roll  up your sleeves  wear a tie do something  to get any type of loose hard object  from buzzing on the instrument  [Music]  okay so what if the problem isn't with  the open strings here it's only when you  fret notes that you have buzz it's  likely that that problem might come from  the bridge side that could mean that  your the saddle which is that white  piece that goes into the bridge  is not high enough  it could also mean that your neck angle  uh is wrong but that's a bit more  complicated so let's hope it's just your  saddle height because again a  do-it-yourself at-home option would be  to just shim it  so again just loosen the strings  all the way down  every one of them  again this doesn't harm your instrument  at all just go all the way down take the  tension off with every string and then  once it's loose enough the white piece  the saddle will just slide right out  then  put the white piece  onto a business card trace it cut that  out put the business card  into the slot  then the saddle and then the strings on  top and tighten it back up you will have  raised  the  height of the saddle and thereby raising  the strings and this might take care of  your buzzing if it doesn't you could put  another business card on there so again  you can shim to make this happen  or in the end probably it's best to  eventually have a luthier or a guitar  repair shop make you a new saddle that's  at the right the right height  [Music]  do  [Music]  you
 

